Title:
AMSI Provider (Persistence via Fake AMSI Provider Registration)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This write-up explains how Windows’ Antimalware Scan Interface (AMSI) can be abused for persistence by registering a malicious third-party AMSI provider. With elevated privileges, an attacker can register a COM in-proc server DLL under `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\AMSI\Providers\{GUID}` so AMSI-aware applications (notably PowerShell) load it during scanning. The provided PoC provider implements `IAntimalwareProvider::Scan()` and triggers code execution when specific strings are observed in PowerShell content, enabling stealthy “keyword-triggered” execution and potential C2 beacons. The post also covers defensive visibility: registry auditing (e.g., Security Event ID 4663), process creation for `regsvr32` (4688), and Sysmon ImageLoad (Event ID 7) for unsigned DLLs loaded by PowerShell. It includes a Sigma rule to detect suspicious AMSI provider/CLSID registrations and recommends enabling AMSI provider signing checks (supported since Windows 10 1903 but not default). Useful for red teams simulating persistence and blue teams building detections around uncommon but viable COM/registry-based persistence.

Technical Focus:
- AMSI architecture and `IAntimalwareProvider` COM interface
- Persistence via AMSI provider registration (`HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\AMSI\Providers`)
- COM InProcServer32 registration and `regsvr32`-based DLL registration
- PowerShell AMSI scanning flow and content-triggered execution
- Windows/Sysmon telemetry: 4663 (registry), 4688 (process), Sysmon 7 (ImageLoad)
- Detection engineering: Sigma + registry auditing + Authenticode signature checks

Use Cases:
- Simulate high-privilege persistence using AMSI provider registration in purple-team exercises
- Build detections for new AMSI provider registrations and suspicious COM InProcServer32 entries
- Hunt for unsigned/untrusted DLLs loaded into `powershell.exe` at startup
- Validate registry auditing coverage for `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\AMSI\Providers`
- Test hardening by enabling/enforcing AMSI provider signing checks

Keywords:
AMSI, IAntimalwareProvider, IAmsiStream, PowerShell, persistence, COM, InProcServer32, CLSID, regsvr32, HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\AMSI\Providers, registry auditing, Event ID 4663, Event ID 4688, Sysmon Event ID 7, ImageLoad, Authenticode, unsigned DLL, Sigma rule, Windows 10 1903 signing check, T1545.015